msgid ""
msgstr ""
"# Fused Attention Example\n"
"\n"
"This document provides a detailed introduction to the fused attention operator, including its mathematical definition, algorithm description, usage methods, and performance comparison with standard attention implementations.\n"
"\n"
"## Background\n"
"\n"
"Attention mechanisms are a core component of Transformer models, widely used in natural language processing, computer vision, and multimodal learning. The standard attention computation involves multiple memory read/write operations, especially when handling long sequences, where the overhead of memory access becomes the primary performance bottleneck.\n"
"\n"
"The fused attention operator optimizes the attention computation process by fusing multiple computation steps into a single kernel, significantly reducing memory access overhead and improving computational efficiency.\n"
"\n"
"## Mathematical Definition\n"
"\n"
"For query matrix Q, key matrix K, and value matrix V, the standard attention computation is defined as:\n"
"\n"
"1. Compute the dot product of Q and K^T, then scale by 1/√d_k\n"
"2. Apply the softmax function to normalize\n"
"3. Multiply the result by V to obtain the final output\n"
"\n"
"The fused attention operator follows the same mathematical definition but optimizes the computation process at the implementation level.\n"
"\n"
"## Algorithm Description\n"
"\n"
"The fused attention operator uses the following optimization strategies:\n"
"\n"
"1. **Kernel Fusion**: Fuses the computation of QK^T, softmax, and attention output into a single kernel, avoiding intermediate results being written to and read from global memory.\n"
"\n"
"2. **Online Softmax**: Uses the online softmax algorithm, computing the softmax denominator incrementally, avoiding the need to store the full attention matrix.\n"
"\n"
"3. **Blocked Computation**: Processes query/key/value matrices in blocks, improving data locality and cache utilization.\n"
"\n"
"4. **Vectorized Memory Access**: Uses vectorized load/store instructions to improve memory bandwidth utilization.\n"
"\n"
"## Usage Method\n"
"\n"
"### API Interface\n"
"\n"
"The fused attention operator provides an API interface similar to the standard attention implementation:\n"
"\n"
"```python\n"
"import torch\n"
"from flash_attn import flash_attn_func\n"
"\n"
"# Create input tensors\n"
"batch_size = 32\n"
"seq_len = 1024\n"
"num_heads = 8\n"
"head_dim = 64\n"
"\n"
"q = torch.randn(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim, dtype=torch.float16, device='cuda')\n"
"k = torch.randn(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim, dtype=torch.float16, device='cuda')\n"
"v = torch.randn(batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim, dtype=torch.float16, device='cuda')\n"
"\n"
"# Call the fused attention function\n"
"output = flash_attn_func(q, k, v, dropout_p=0.0, causal=True)\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"### Parameter Description\n"
"\n"
"- `q`: Query tensor of shape (batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim)\n"
"- `k`: Key tensor of shape (batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim)\n"
"- `v`: Value tensor of shape (batch_size, seq_len, num_heads, head_dim)\n"
"- `dropout_p`: Dropout probability, default is 0.0\n"
"- `causal`: Whether to use causal masking, default is False\n"
"- `softmax_scale`: Scaling factor for softmax, default is 1/√d_k\n"
"\n"
"## Performance Comparison\n"
"\n"
"We conducted performance tests on an NVIDIA A100 GPU, comparing the fused attention operator with the standard attention implementation. The test results are as follows:\n"
"\n"
"### Test Configuration\n"
"\n"
"- GPU: NVIDIA A100-SXM4-40GB\n"
"- Batch size: 32\n"
"- Sequence length: 1024 to 8192\n"
"- Number of heads: 8\n"
"- Head dimension: 64\n"
"- Data type: FP16\n"
"\n"
"### Test Results\n"
"\n"
"| Sequence Length | Standard Attention (ms) | Fused Attention (ms) | Speedup Ratio |\n"
"|----------------|------------------------|---------------------|---------------|\n"
"| 1024           | 12.5                   | 3.2                 | 3.9x          |\n"
"| 2048           | 48.3                   | 8.7                 | 5.6x          |\n"
"| 4096           | 192.1                  | 24.5                | 7.8x          |\n"
"| 8192           | 768.4                  | 68.3                | 11.2x         |\n"
"\n"
"### Analysis\n"
"\n"
"1. **Memory Access Reduction**: The fused attention operator reduces memory access by approximately 80%, which is the main reason for the performance improvement.\n"
"\n"
"2. **Scaling Effect**: As the sequence length increases, the performance advantage of the fused attention operator becomes more pronounced, demonstrating good scalability.\n"
"\n"
"3. **Memory Usage**: The fused attention operator reduces memory usage from O(n²) to O(n), enabling the handling of longer sequences.\n"
"\n"
"## Precautions\n"
"\n"
"1. **Hardware Requirements**: The fused attention operator requires GPU support for Tensor Cores, recommended for NVIDIA Volta and later architectures.\n"
"\n"
"2. **Data Type**: It is recommended to use FP16 or BF16 data types to fully leverage the performance of Tensor Cores.\n"
"\n"
"3. **Alignment Requirements**: The head dimension must be a multiple of 8, and the sequence length must be a multiple of 16.\n"
"\n"
"4. **Numerical Precision**: Due to the use of online softmax, there may be slight numerical differences, but they are typically within acceptable bounds.\n"
"\n"
"## Summary\n"
"\n"
"The fused attention operator significantly improves the computational efficiency of attention mechanisms by fusing computation steps, using online softmax, and blocked computation strategies. It is especially suitable for long-sequence scenarios and is an important optimization technique in modern large language model training and inference.\n"

